w. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\nL114: \nL115:   1. 【61†ingonyama-zk】\nL116:   2. 【62†icicle】\nL117: ## Repository navigation\nL118: \nL119:   *   * 【62†Code】\nL120:   * 【63†Issues27 (27)】\nL121:   * 【64†Pull requests22 (22)】\nL122:   * 【65†Actions】\nL123:   * 【66†Projects】\nL124:   * 【67†Security and quality】\nL125:   * 【68†Insights】\nL126: \nL127: More items\nL128: \nL129: # Releases: ingonyama-zk/icicle\nL130: \nL131: 【69†Releases】 【70†Tags】\nL132: \nL133: [Input: Find a release] [Input]\nL134: \nL135: Releases · ingonyama-zk/icicle\nL136: ## Release list\nL137: \nL138:   * 【71†Release v4.0.0】\nL139:   * 【72†Release v3.9.2】\nL140:   * 【73†Release v3.9.1】\nL141:   * 【74†Release v3.9.0】\nL142:   * 【75†Release v3.8.0】\nL143:   * 【76†Release v3.7.1】\nL144:   * 【77†Release v3.7.0】\nL145:   * 【78†Release v3.6.0】\nL146:   * 【79†Release v3.5.0】\nL147:   * 【80†Release v3.4.0】\nL148: \nL149: Jump to release\nL150: \nL151: ## Release v4.0.0\nL152: \nL153: 【81†Release v4.0.0】\nL154: \nL155: 【82†Latest】\nL156: \nL157: Compare\nL158: \nL159: # Choose a tag to compare\nL160: \nL161: ## Sorry, something went wrong.\nL162: \nL163: Filter\nL164: \nL165: Loading [Input]\nL166: \nL167: ## Sorry, something went wrong.\nL168: ### Uh oh!\nL169: \nL170: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL171: \nL172: ## No results found\nL173: \nL174: 【70†View all tags】\nL175: \nL176: 【83†Image: @github-actions†avatars.githubusercontent.com】 【84†github-actions】 released this 11 Jul 05:36\nL177: \nL178: 【85†v4.0.0】\nL179: \nL180: 【86†`6c77775`】\nL181: \nL182: # ICICLE v4.0.0 Release Notes\nL183: ## 🎉 Major Release: ICICLE v4.0.0\nL184: \nL185: We're excited to announce the release of ICICLE v4.0.0! This major version introduces significant improvements to the API design, making it more intuitive, object-oriented, and developer-friendly across all supported languages.\nL186: ## 🚀 Key Highlights\nL187:   * Object-Oriented Field API: Complete redesign of field operations from static methods to instance methods\nL188:   * Method Chaining Support: Write more concise and expressive code with method chaining\nL189:   * Improved Rust API: Better trait design with standard operator overloading\nL190:   * Enhanced Program API: Reorganized Program module with better type safety\nL191:   * Backward Compatibility: Comprehensive migration guides for v3 to v4 transition\nL192:   * Post Quantum: Implemented a NIST FIPS203 (ML-KEM) protocol on CUDA, fully open-source\nL193:   * Lattice Based Cryptography: Add support for Lattice based cryptography, with integer ring support and a full LaBRADOR prover (soon announced)\nL194: ## 🔄 Breaking Changes\nL195: \nL196: ### C++ API Changes\nL197: \nL198: #### Field Arithmetic Operations\nL199: \nL200: All field operations have been converted from static methods to instance methods\nL201: Check the 【87†migration page†dev.ingonyama.com】 for more details\nL202: \nL203: ### Rust API Changes\nL204: \nL205: #### Arithmetic Trait Redesign\nL206: \nL207: The `FieldImpl` trait has been deprecated and split into focused, orthogonal traits\nL208: ## What's Changed\nL209:   * Negacyclic NTT for polynomial rings by 【88†@yshekel】 in 【89†#930】\nL210:   * Update README.md by 【90†@Shalmoni】 in 【91†#931】\nL211:   * Fixed devdocs link licensing by 【90†@Shalmoni】 in 【92†#933】\nL212:   * Fix collab instructions for version mismatch error by 【93†@jeremyfelder】 in 【94†#935】\nL213:   * Feat: generate JL-projection matrix rows by 【88†@yshekel】 in 【95†#936】\nL214:   * Curve Montgomery Conversion Fix for Host Memory by 【96†@emirsoyturk】 in 【97†#938】\nL215:   * [FIX] Invalid memory access causes hang by 【93†@jeremyfelder】 in 【98†#939】\nL216:   * PQC ML-KEM by 【99†@nonam3e】 and 【100†@dorshmil】 in 【101†#932】\nL217:   * Add PQC documentation to 3.9.0 by 【102†@LeonHibnik】 in 【103†#940】\nL218:   * Changed broken link on readme by 【90†@Shalmoni】 in 【104†#942】\nL219:   * [FEAT] Rust Polynomial-Ring trait and NegacyclicNTT by 【88†@yshekel】 in 【105†#943】\nL220:   * [FEAT] Decompose Polynomial-rings by 【88†@yshekel】 in 【106†#945】\nL221:   * [FEAT] JL-projection Rust wrapper by 【88†@yshekel】 in 【107†#949】\nL222:   * [FEAT] MatMul API by 【108†@lnm-at-work】 in 【109†#871】\nL223:   * [FEAT] Expose Sumcheck protocol challenge vector by 【102†@LeonHibnik】 in 【110†#946】\nL224:   * Timur/pqc go wrappers by 【99†@nonam3e】 in 【111†#944】\nL225:   * [FEAT] Add flattening utilities for polynomial slices into scalar slices by 【88†@yshekel】 in 【112†#952】\nL226:   * [CI] Separate testing by feature by 【93†@jeremyfelder】 in 【113†#913】\nL227:   * [FEAT] Add reinterpretation utilities and polynomial vector operations by 【88†@yshekel】 in 【114†#954】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEquality-factored sum-check - Akita Book (https://layerzero-labs.github.io/akita/foundations/eq-factored-sumcheck.html)\nciteturn1view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://layerzero-labs.github.io/akita/foundations/eq-factored-sumcheck.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 107\nL0: ## Keyboard shortcuts\nL1: \nL2: Press `←` or `→` to navigate between chapters\nL3: \nL4: Press `S` or `/` to search in the book\nL5: \nL6: Press `?` to show this help\nL7: \nL8: Press `Esc` to hide this help\nL9: \nL10: 【0†iframe】\nL11: \nL12: # Akita Book\nL13: # 【1†Equality-factored sum-check】\nL14: \nL15: Many Akita identities give extra weight to one point τ through the equality polynomial:\nL16: \nL17: T=x∈{0,1}n∑​eq(τ,x)p(x).\nL18: \nL19: Ordinary sum-check can prove this claim. Equality-factored sum-check proves the same claim with smaller round messages and a smaller prover table. The saving comes from treating the known equality factor separately from the polynomial p that carries the protocol data.\nL20: Read 【2†Multilinear extensions and sum-check】 first if the ordinary round check is not yet familiar.\nL21: ## 【3†The equality factor splits by coordinate】\nL22: \nL23: For one coordinate, define\nL24: \nL25: eq(τ i​,X)=(1−τ i​)(1−X)+τ i​X.\nL26: \nL27: This is a linear polynomial in X. Across all coordinates,\nL28: \nL29: eq(τ,x)=i=0∏n−1​eq(τ i​,x i​).\nL30: \nL31: After the first j variables have been bound to challenges r 0​,…,r j−1​, their equality contribution is one scalar:\nL32: \nL33: γ j​=i=0∏j−1​eq(τ i​,r i​).\nL34: \nL35: The corresponding full round polynomial has the linear factor\nL36: \nL37: l j​(X)=γ j​eq(τ j​,X).\nL38: The remaining coordinates stay inside the Boolean sum. The full round polynomial therefore factors as\nL39: \nL40: s j​(X)=l j​(X)q j​(X),\nL41: \nL42: where q j​ is the round polynomial formed from p and the equality factors for the still unbound coordinates. Equality-factored sum-check normalizes the already-bound scalar γ j​ out of the running claim. Its round identity therefore uses eq(τ j​,X) directly rather than carrying l j​(X) from one round to the next.\nL43: ## 【4†Omit the constant coefficient】\nL44: \nL45: Suppose q j​ has degree at most d. The full polynomial s j​=l j​q j​ can have degree d+1 because l j​ is linear. The verifier already knows l j​, so Akita sends coefficients of q j​ instead of coefficients of s j​.\nL46: \nL47: There is one more saving. Write\nL48: \nL49: q j​(X)=q 0​+q 1​X+q 2​X 2+⋯+q d​X d\nL50: \nL51: The normalized current claim obeys\nL52: \nL53: T j​=(1−τ j​)q j​(0)+τ j​q j​(1)=q 0​+τ j​(q 1​+q 2​+⋯+q d​).\nL54: The coefficient of q 0​ is exactly one, independently of τ j​. Akita does not send that coefficient. An equality-factored round message stores\nL55: \nL56:     `[q1, q2, ..., qd]\nL57:     `\nL58: \nL59: For the common case where p contributes degree two, ordinary sum-check would send three coefficients for the degree-three product s j​. The factored message sends two coefficients for the degree-two inner polynomial q j​.\nL60: `jolt_poly::OmittedConstantPoly` stores this round message, and `akita-serialization` encodes it on the wire. A degree-d inner polynomial contributes exactly d field elements. Thus every deployed Akita Stage 1 round, whose inner degree is positive, keeps the same message width; the valid degree-zero case needs no transmitted coefficient.\nL61: ## 【5†The verifier avoids division】\nL62: \nL63: The verifier recovers the omitted coefficient by subtraction:\nL64: \nL65: q 0​=T j​−τ j​(q 1​+q 2​+⋯+q d​).\nL66: \nL67: After sampling r j​, it advances the normalized claim as\nL68: \nL69: T j+1​=q j​(r j​)=q 0​+q 1​r j​+q 2​r j 2​+⋯+q d​r j d​.\nL70: \nL71: No inverse or accumulated claim scale appears, and the formula remains valid when τ j​=0 or when an earlier equality evaluation vanishes. The final verifier check compares T n​ directly with the expected folded oracle value.\nL72: The public function `advance_eq_factored_claim` in `akita-sumcheck/src/single.rs` implements these equations directly. The direct `prove_eq_factored_sumcheck` and `verify_eq_factored_sumcheck` functions both call it, so transcript replay and proof generation cannot drift onto different update rules.\nL73: ## 【6†Avoiding a full equality table】\nL74: \nL75: A direct prover can materialize all 2 n values eq(τ,x) and fold that table in every round. Akita uses `GruenSplitEq` instead.\nL76: \nL77: It divides the unbound coordinates, other than the current one, into two halves. For each half it caches equality tables for its remaining suffixes. At a round, the equality weight for table index k is reconstructed as\nL78: \nL79: E first​[k low​]⋅E second​[k high​].\nL80: The specialized driver reads the current coordinate τ j​ and the remaining tables, then pops one cached table level after binding a challenge. `GruenSplitEq` also tracks γ j​ because other, ordinary sum-check paths use it to reconstruct the full polynomial l j​(X)q j​(X); the normalized equality-factored verifier does not multiply its claim by this scalar.\nL81: Each half has about n/2 coordinates. The largest cached table therefore has about 2 n/2 entries rather than 2 n. Including all smaller cached levels changes only the constant factor. This is a substantial memory reduction for large sum-checks.\nL82: \nL83: `GruenSplitEq` lives in `akita-algebra/src/split_eq.rs`. Its tests compare every reconstructed weight and every fold against a fully materialized equality table.\nL84: ## 【7†When this form applies】\nL85: \nL86: The optimization applies when the verifier knows a common equality factor for the sum-check instance. It is especially useful for claims built around one verifier point τ.\nL87: Instances that share the same factor can also share the specialized round logic. A mixed batch with different equality factors cannot omit the same inner coefficient under one common round equation. Such a batch uses ordinary compressed sum-check messages instead. The prover may still use split equality tables internally to save memory.\nL88: This distinction matters in review. The equality-table representation is a prover implementation choice. Constant-coefficient omission is specific to the normalized equality-factored identity above; ordinary sum-check still omits the linear coefficient. Changing between the two proof messages requires the matching verifier equation.\nL89: ## 【8†Where to inspect the implementation】\nL90: \nL91: Rule  | Implementation\nL92: --- | ---\nL93: Equality polynomial definition and tables  | `akita-algebra/src/eq_poly.rs`\nL94: Split equality state and suffix caches  | `akita-algebra/src/split_eq.rs`\nL95: Factored prover interface  | `akita-sumcheck/src/traits.rs`\nL96: Factored round message and proof encoding  | `akita-sumcheck/src/types.rs`\nL97: Direct prover/verifier functions and normalized claim update  | `akita-sumcheck/src/single.rs`\nL98: \nL99: For a review, check these facts together:\nL100:   1. The challenge order used by τ, the data table, and `GruenSplitEq` agrees.\nL101:   2. The declared degree bounds apply to the inner polynomial q j​, not the product l j​q j​.\nL102:   3. The proof shape fixes the number of rounds and stored coefficients before decoding.\nL103:   4. Prover and verifier use the same τ j​ and challenge in every update.\nL104:   5. The final comparison uses the normalized oracle value, without an accumulated equality scale.\n"